WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:22.800
Conflicts of Interest for the NHS

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:32.040
Bruce Keogh: You will recall that in 2015,
we set out guidance for commissioners on conflicts

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:40.160
of interest, with particular respect to CCGs,
and at our Board meeting in March 2016, we

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:51.470
agreed to extend that to a much wider audience
across the NHS. Ian will describe the process

00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:59.350
that we followed for that, and which you chaired,
but it was driven I think by a number of influences

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:06.679
which are beginning to emerge, and in no specific
order, there have been changes in the approach

00:01:06.679 --> 00:01:12.090
to this in the United States, driven in part
by the Sunshine Act. That has driven changes

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:17.790
with the pharmaceutical industry and the medical
device industry. There is growing public and

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:25.770
media interest in conflicts of interest, and,
of course, there are also issues related to

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:32.050
procurement rules and transparency. All of
these things have come together, I think,

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:38.250
to mean that the time is right for the NHS
to have clarity over how we approach conflicts

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:40.660
of interest.

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:47.750
The document which you have before you, from
my point of view, I think has probably three

00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:54.180
strengths. The first lies in its simplicity;
it attempts to set out a number of common

00:01:54.180 --> 00:02:00.560
issues, to define a set of principles which
in turn will translate into a set of behaviours

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:07.709
that are expected of both individuals and
organisations. The second thing is, it is

00:02:07.709 --> 00:02:14.410
an attempt to align with professional guidance
that already exists, with principles of public

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:23.810
life, with the Competition and Markets Authority
guidelines, and also with changing pharma

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:31.340
and medical device guidelines, as they start
to emerge. The third thing I think it does

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:40.480
is, it tries to acknowledge the evolving environment
in which this process has happened, in part

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:47.060
driven, again, by the pharmaceutical and device
industry, and also by some emerging evidence

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:58.250
that the relationships that are developed
between the pharma industry and people conducting

00:02:58.250 --> 00:03:05.360
trials, the very nature and construct of those
may actually in some instances influence the

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:14.000
outcomes of trials. This is a complex and
changing environment, so this is a first attempt,

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:20.160
really, for the NHS to define a set of principles,
rules, that will underpin a set of behaviours,

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:28.600
which should, if done properly, protect both
organisations and individuals from imputation

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:36.190
of false motive. I think we need to be clear
that this environment will continue to change;

00:03:36.190 --> 00:03:42.720
this is not the final word, and I would imagine
we will go through an iterative process as

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:49.780
we seek to understand exactly what conflicts
of interest mean for people in the NHS.

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:57.830
Ian Dodge: Thank you very much, Bruce, for
laying out the context very clearly. I think

00:03:57.830 --> 00:04:03.680
there are four purposes behind this work,
one of which is protecting the taxpayer’s

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:12.510
interests; secondly, maintaining public trust;
thirdly, supporting NHS employers; and fourthly,

00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:20.150
protecting staff themselves. The way in which
we sought to achieve those objectives is by

00:04:20.150 --> 00:04:29.560
articulating and codifying what is good practice,
so that we make the task of dealing with conflicts

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:35.490
of interest simpler for people in the NHS,
and that, rather than everyone having to invent

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:42.570
for themselves marginally different policies
and ways of complying with those policies,

00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:51.090
we reduce the transaction costs and burdens
through a process of standardisation and simplification.

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:58.819
As you have explained, Bruce, work on conflicts
of interest is not an unexplored terrain,

00:04:58.819 --> 00:05:05.379
and this most recent process builds on three
things. It builds on the first-ever statutory

00:05:05.379 --> 00:05:11.050
guidance for CCGs, which we developed particularly
within the context of the heightened risk

00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:21.889
associated with the delegation of the commissioning
of primary medical care services to CCGs.

00:05:21.889 --> 00:05:28.870
It picks up as well on the changes in the
NHS Standard Contract, which now apply. It

00:05:28.870 --> 00:05:36.289
also builds on the very encouraging work that
has been led by the pharmaceutical industry

00:05:36.289 --> 00:05:43.210
under the auspices of the ABPI, called Disclosure
UK, which was kick-started in the summer of

00:05:43.210 --> 00:05:45.270
last year.

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:52.530
The process we have gone through under Sir&nbsp;Malcolm’s
chairmanship is to convene a broad-based task

00:05:52.530 --> 00:05:59.759
and finish group, comprised of key members
of the professions, the national arm’s-length

00:05:59.759 --> 00:06:08.189
bodies, representative bodies, and, indeed,
industry representatives. We produced in September

00:06:08.189 --> 00:06:16.699
of last year a set of proposals for public
consultation, and then we have gone through

00:06:16.699 --> 00:06:26.289
a process of very careful consideration of
the 250 responses that were submitted, in

00:06:26.289 --> 00:06:35.849
the light of which, the task and finish group
has decided upon a number of I think important

00:06:35.849 --> 00:06:40.689
modifications in the light of the constructive
challenge that is received, and I just wanted

00:06:40.689 --> 00:06:49.139
to place on record obviously the thanks to
those who have responded to the consultation

00:06:49.139 --> 00:06:55.009
to help us improve the final product.

00:06:55.009 --> 00:07:03.759
Today, this morning, we have now published
revised guidance of that group, and it also

00:07:03.759 --> 00:07:15.210
includes an analysis of the consultation responses.
We have set out clear proposals on gifts,

00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:23.479
on hospitality, on employment, on donations,
and I particularly wanted to highlight three

00:07:23.479 --> 00:07:33.610
areas where there is some change from the
original proposition. First of all, on the

00:07:33.610 --> 00:07:39.889
scope, we have clarified that this guidance
applies specifically to NHS providers; we

00:07:39.889 --> 00:07:46.129
commend it to other organisations who are
delivering service under contract to the NHS,

00:07:46.129 --> 00:07:52.080
but we have been keen to make sure that we
do not impose disproportionate burden, particularly

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:57.970
on small organisations with their own separate
governance arrangements, and, indeed, the

00:07:57.970 --> 00:08:03.229
professional codes of conduct that provide
additional public protection, because we have

00:08:03.229 --> 00:08:08.809
not wanted to impose unnecessary burdens on
those small providers.

00:08:08.809 --> 00:08:15.039
Secondly, we heard very clear feedback that
our definition of senior staff was pitched

00:08:15.039 --> 00:08:20.879
at slightly the wrong level, and we have therefore
amended that to apply to band 8Ds and above

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:22.780
rather than 8Cs.

00:08:23.540 --> 00:08:31.480
Thirdly, on clinical private practice, we
have heard some support for the underlying

00:08:31.490 --> 00:08:39.390
principle, but also a very clear message that
the specific way in which we were proposing

00:08:39.390 --> 00:08:45.770
to capture that principle was not the best
route to proceed. What we are doing in this

00:08:45.770 --> 00:08:50.670
guidance is, we are setting something out
which is comparable to the way in which other

00:08:50.670 --> 00:08:59.760
non-clinical staff have to declare their time
commitment to outside interests.

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:06.490
The guidance will come into effect on 1st
June. We are developing with our partners

00:09:06.490 --> 00:09:14.730
a suite of templates and standard material
to make it easy for NHS organisations to comply,

00:09:14.730 --> 00:09:23.269
and we will keep under review the effect.
With that, Chairman, I invite the Board to

00:09:23.269 --> 00:09:28.109
fully endorse the guidance.
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much, Bruce

00:09:28.109 --> 00:09:33.900
and Ian. I had the privilege of chairing the
task and finish group. I would just actually

00:09:33.910 --> 00:09:39.630
say, in my experience, it was very unusual
to have such a coterminosity of views between

00:09:39.630 --> 00:09:44.470
the industry and the NHS, and I think with
the regulators on board as well, and the other

00:09:44.470 --> 00:09:47.959
arm’s-length bodies, it was possible to
do a job that we could not have done on our

00:09:47.959 --> 00:09:56.560
own, and to really try to create a regime
of relatively uniform principles; actually,

00:09:56.560 --> 00:10:01.170
quite uniform principles, but recently uniform
rules as well, across a very large sector

00:10:01.170 --> 00:10:04.710
of activity, because there is no point in
having one set of rules for the NHS and one

00:10:04.710 --> 00:10:09.730
set of rules for the industry, and creating
not only confusion but also opportunity for

00:10:09.730 --> 00:10:14.650
avoidance, so I think it has been an important
exercise for us.

00:10:22.700 --> 00:10:27.780
Wendy Becker: Obviously, a critical context,
and not a new one, and a very extensive process.

00:10:27.790 --> 00:10:31.620
I would be curious at the end of all of that,
what do you think are going to be the biggest

00:10:31.620 --> 00:10:35.780
changes coming out of the implementation of
this work?

00:10:39.640 --> 00:10:48.720
Bruce Keogh: It will be I think a 
set of behaviours which I think will mean

00:10:48.730 --> 00:10:58.269
greater transparency, and I think organisations
will focus I think more clearly on where conflicts

00:10:58.269 --> 00:11:04.120
of interest may occur, in a way that will
protect both them and their staff from those

00:11:04.120 --> 00:11:11.399
potential accusations.
Ian Dodge: May I add, I think what this process

00:11:11.399 --> 00:11:16.310
is doing is shining a light on an area that
people are conscious is something that needs

00:11:16.310 --> 00:11:23.750
to be addressed, but had not necessarily given
it the attention that it deserves. What we,

00:11:23.750 --> 00:11:29.220
I think, have done is found a way of making
it easy for organisations and individuals

00:11:29.220 --> 00:11:35.690
to actually follow good practice, and then
we will see during the course of the next

00:11:35.690 --> 00:11:38.959
months the extent to which we have had that
effect.

00:11:38.959 --> 00:11:48.610
David Roberts: Clearly a very in-depth, very
solid piece of work. I guess I am wondering

00:11:48.610 --> 00:11:54.330
a bit about, how will we know if it has had
the desired effect? You mentioned that this

00:11:54.330 --> 00:12:03.180
was on a journey, so what is the review process
to be able to look at that desired effect

00:12:03.180 --> 00:12:08.829
against the objectives and then determine
whether further changes are needed?

00:12:10.040 --> 00:12:16.900
Ian Dodge: One of the main ways in which we
will know is the public material that is made

00:12:16.910 --> 00:12:25.110
available from NHS organisations, both the
CCGs and, indeed, the providers, that is available

00:12:25.110 --> 00:12:29.699
to the public, that shows how well they are
complying with the guidance. What we will

00:12:29.699 --> 00:12:39.910
be doing is looking and keeping under review
what that is telling us. If I could pick up

00:12:39.910 --> 00:12:49.660
the example on CCG statutory guidance, where
the process pre-dates this work, one of the

00:12:49.660 --> 00:12:54.380
things we have set up really clearly there
is the requirement to publish policies and

00:12:54.380 --> 00:13:02.339
processes. Those then have been subject to
audit by the CCGs themselves, and it is possible

00:13:02.339 --> 00:13:10.639
for the public to actually go onto the NHS
websites and see the extent to which those

00:13:10.639 --> 00:13:17.279
have been effectively followed, David. I think
we will need to see how the transparency is

00:13:17.279 --> 00:13:22.899
working, and then what more, if anything,
we need to do to support NHS organisations

00:13:22.899 --> 00:13:25.740
to be able to adhere to the rules effectively.

00:13:25.740 --> 00:13:32.910
David Roberts: Ian, will we be expecting,
as we did with CCGs and have put in place,

00:13:32.910 --> 00:13:40.560
that there will be internal and external audit
review of whether the processes have been

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:45.500
complied with, or are we leaving that to the
judgement of individual organisations?

00:13:45.500 --> 00:13:53.160
Ian Dodge: We have taken a really clear line
for CCGs because of the particular risk there,

00:13:53.160 --> 00:14:00.939
but for the other organisations, we are not,
I believe, mandating formal audit, but we

00:14:00.939 --> 00:14:04.639
will be keeping the process under review.

00:14:07.880 --> 00:14:12.000
Malcolm Grant: Clearly, guidance like this
is never self-implementing, tempting though

00:14:12.009 --> 00:14:18.209
it always would be to believe that, from where
we sit. I think if you read the responses

00:14:18.209 --> 00:14:21.660
– and there are extensive responses, it
is a very good summary, actually, in the papers

00:14:21.660 --> 00:14:26.920
the Board has – I would not say there is
a ravenous appetite for this, but there is

00:14:26.920 --> 00:14:33.899
certainly quite a lot of desire to have a
uniform system which is more uniformly implemented

00:14:33.899 --> 00:14:41.790
and observed. I think there is another issue,
which is that, as we are bringing about a

00:14:41.790 --> 00:14:47.260
series of changes across the NHS which involve
new models of care, new models of partnership,

00:14:47.260 --> 00:14:53.380
new relationships between different organisations,
the premium on identifying where potential

00:14:53.380 --> 00:14:58.389
conflicts might occur, recognising them, and
recording them upfront, I think is a very

00:14:58.389 --> 00:15:04.410
important safeguard which gives a larger measure
of security to those who are on our behalf

00:15:04.410 --> 00:15:10.129
entering into these new ventures. I think
that Ian is absolutely right; we need to be

00:15:10.129 --> 00:15:15.290
overseeing what is happening. We have much
more acute instances, obviously, within CCGs,

00:15:15.290 --> 00:15:22.550
but more generally, I think this is an opportunity
to support people and not merely to have this

00:15:22.550 --> 00:15:26.640
perceived as yet another bureaucratic burden;
it is emphatically not that.

00:15:29.260 --> 00:15:32.879
Moira Gibb: I was just going to say that,
I think what we are trying to ensure is that

00:15:32.879 --> 00:15:39.819
the public can be reassured that organisations,
individuals and arrangements are more trustworthy,

00:15:39.819 --> 00:15:47.990
and I do want to underscore David’s point
about audit in due course taking an interest

00:15:47.990 --> 00:15:53.059
in these. I think we should also recognise
that it is unlikely that the public will have

00:15:53.059 --> 00:16:00.620
more trust, because the very nature of making
it transparent encourages people to think

00:16:00.620 --> 00:16:07.089
there is something going wrong. In terms of
Wendy’s question about what change, we certainly

00:16:07.089 --> 00:16:11.769
should not seek to measure it in terms of
public trust, because I think that will not

00:16:11.769 --> 00:16:15.579
be changed by this, or might in fact go in
the wrong direction.

00:16:16.120 --> 00:16:20.860
Malcolm Grant: I think that is an absolutely
valid point. If you take the example of Disclosure

00:16:20.870 --> 00:16:28.839
UK, where there has been quite a bit of resistance
to clinicians and others declaring the payments

00:16:28.839 --> 00:16:34.660
that they have received from the pharmaceutical
industry, the danger of not declaring it is

00:16:34.660 --> 00:16:42.759
actually that when it is discovered and becomes
public, then there is an opportunity for imputation

00:16:42.759 --> 00:16:48.360
of false motive. I think the critical thing
that has come about here is to be able to

00:16:48.360 --> 00:16:53.480
defend the fact that clinicians are not receiving
bribes from the pharmaceutical industry; they

00:16:53.480 --> 00:16:58.439
are actually performing valuable services,
and this needs to be reflected in the way

00:16:58.439 --> 00:17:05.189
in which the receipt is declared. We would
push very hard for 100% observance of the

00:17:05.189 --> 00:17:12.100
rules that have been introduced by the ABPI
under Disclosure UK, and it I think has the

00:17:12.100 --> 00:17:18.700
dual purpose of being able to be clear about
why money is being paid, and also being able

00:17:18.700 --> 00:17:23.280
to be clear that there is nothing being hidden
from public view.

00:17:27.920 --> 00:17:33.800
Ian Dodge: In response to your observation,
Moira, I think there is a phasing point here,

00:17:33.800 --> 00:17:40.250
that as we end up achieving greater coverage
of disclosure, you are indeed right that there

00:17:40.250 --> 00:17:47.120
is a greater risk of an undermining of public
trust. However, as that process completes

00:17:47.120 --> 00:17:50.940
– and as Bruce says, there is a journey
we are going on – actually, the intention

00:17:50.940 --> 00:17:56.310
is that it will end up providing a greater
degree of certainty for the public. I think

00:17:56.310 --> 00:18:00.610
that is likely to be the outcome, but it will
be a non-linear journey.

00:18:03.620 --> 00:18:06.260
Malcolm Grant: Yes, I do not know of many
linear journeys in any of the work that we

00:18:06.270 --> 00:18:12.420
do, so that is not a surprising conclusion
for us.

00:18:12.420 --> 00:18:21.190
On that basis, may I invite the Board to endorse
the publication of the guidance? We note that

00:18:21.190 --> 00:18:26.390
there will be a lot of interest in its implementation,
and we should create an audit trail to ensure

00:18:26.390 --> 00:18:32.250
that it has the effect that we wish is to
have. Is that agreed? Okay.

